      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2012 Canadian Ophthalmological Society. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To evaluate the efficacy of amniotic membrane transplantation (AMT) to improve the outcomes of acute Pseudomonas keratitis as compared with a control group."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "Prospective interventional case series with retrospective controls."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
            "content": "We studied 14 eyes with Pseudomonas keratitis as the AMT group and 11 eyes with Pseudomonas keratitis as the control group."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Eyes in the AMT group were treated with antibiotic therapy followed by single-layer AMT at 2 to 3 days. Eyes in the control group received only antibiotic therapy. Patients were followed for 11.1 ± 2.4 months."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "In the AMT group, pain significantly decreased from a mean score of 2.4 ± 0.5 preoperatively to 1.1 ± 0.9 at day 2 postoperatively (p < 0.001). Corneal epithelial defects healed completely within 13.2 ± 2.6 days in the AMT group compared with 15.5 ± 3.4 days in the control group (p = 0.07). At final follow-up visits, the sizes of corneal opacity and deep neovascularization were not different between the 2 groups. However, the mean score for density of the corneal opacity was significantly less in the AMT group compared with the control group (2.1 ± 0.4 vs 2.5 ± 0.7, respectively, p = 0.04). Although the best corrected visual acuity using hard contact lenses was not different between the 2 groups, uncorrected visual acuity was better in the AMT group (0.45 ± 0.22 logMAR) than in the control group (0.71 ± 0.32 logMAR, p = 0.03). No patient in either group developed significant corneal thinning or perforation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "AMT in acute Pseudomonas keratitis was associated with immediate pain relief, less density of the final corneal opacity, and better uncorrected visual acuity at the final follow-up visit."
          }
        ]
      },
